<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epirus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epirus is a mountainous region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindus">Pindus</a> range and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>, divided today between north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. In antiquity it was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> and other Greek-speaking tribes, whose kingdom reached its height under Pyrrhus. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the core of the Despotate of Epirus, and later of Ali Pasha's domain centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epirus.jpg" alt="Epirus" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Kiepert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epirus_antiquus_tabula.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Classical Epirus lay roughly between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindus">Pindus Mountains</a> in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a> in the west, and between the Acroceraunian Mountains in the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a> in the south. The region is rugged, with steep limestone ridges parallel to the sea, so that the valleys are mostly suited to pasture rather than large-scale farming. The highest point is Mount Smolikas, at 2,637 m. The main river is the Vjosë, and the only large lake is Lake Pamvotis, on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> stands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early 1st millennium BCE the fourteen Epirote tribes, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaonians">Chaonians</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thesprotians">Thesprotians</a>, spoke a strong west Greek dialect. They lived in small villages rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, and their region was a frontier contested with Illyrian peoples. Epirus had religious importance through the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodona">Dodona</a>, regarded as second only to Delphi. Thucydides called the Epirotes barbarians, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and Pausanias described them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. From 370 BCE the Molossian Aeacidae dynasty built a centralised state, and in 359 BCE the Molossian princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and became the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</p><p>Epirus became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in 146 BCE, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> it became a separate province. After 1204 it was the centre of the Despotate of Epirus, a successor state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and in the 15th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> conquered it. In the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-pasha-of-yanina">Ali Pasha of Tepelena</a>, who became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> in 1788, ruled a de facto independent Pashalik of Yanina, until the Ottoman government reasserted control in 1821. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a> southern Epirus, including Ioannina, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, while northern Epirus was assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The region is divided between the Greek region of Epirus, whose seat is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>, and the Albanian counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjirokaster">Gjirokastër</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a>. The Greek region covers only part of classical Epirus. Livestock keeping is the main industry, and the Vikos Gorge is the centrepiece of the Vikos-Aoös National Park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Epirus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…its Balkan neighbours, shaped the wars that ended Ottoman rule in Europe, and its territory grew from the narrow state of 1832 to include the Ionian Islands (1864), Thessaly and Arta (1881), Macedonia, <b>Epirus</b>, Crete and the eastern Aegean islands (1912–13), Western Thrace (1920) and the Dodecanese (1947).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom first lay along the Haliacmon and Loudias rivers north of Mount Olympus and along the coast of the Thermaic Gulf, bordered by <b>Epirus</b>, Illyria, Paeonia, Thrace and Thessaly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Despotate of <b>Epirus</b> under Theodore Komnenos Doukas captured and executed Peter of Courtenay, took Thessalonica in 1224 and conquered Thrace in 1225–26, reaching Constantinople itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Squeezed between the Despotate of the Morea, Venice, the Tocco rulers of <b>Epirus</b> and Ottoman raiders, he lost ground steadily.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Asen II (r. 1218–1241) won the Battle of Klokotnitsa on 9 March 1230, capturing Theodore Komnenos Doukas, ruler of <b>Epirus</b> and Thessalonica, and bringing much of Thrace, Macedonia and Albania under Bulgarian rule, as he proclaimed in an inscription in the Church of the Forty Martyrs in Tarnovo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Dušan seized Thessaly, Albania, <b>Epirus</b> and most of Macedonia from the Byzantines, winning by sieges rather than field battles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
